From article <1138@cf-cm.UUCP>, by awn@computing-maths.cardiff.ac.uk (Andrew Wilson):
> We've managed to compile the game (after de-ANSIIfying the code) on a
> SUN 3/60 network.
> 
> and the server:
> 	netmud source.db output.db
> part of the package seems to work OK.
> 
> How do you actually play the thing though?  Typing in things to the server
> has no effect (I didn't figure it would anyway) Are we missing a User-Interface
> or something.

The server program monitors a TCP/IP port, so you can't type things
into it directly. Type
telnet (your computer name) 4201

to connect to and play the game.  (You can change the port number
used by giving a number other than 4201 as a third argument to netmud.
You should probably run the server in the background with the command line

netmud source.db output.db [port] >& mud.log &

which will send the server log to the file mud.log.
In the "standard distribution" is a file restart-cmu (which you can
rename, clearly) which does the above (not in the background, though)
along with some other basic db management.

> 
> Also, in Makefile the TAGS target calls the command "etags *.c *.h".  We've
> never heard of etags here so what does that do??
> 
etags make a TAGS file which allows emacs users to jump automatically
to any given function or useful definition in the source.  If you use
gnuemacs, look in the info section on tags.

